WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.590
Every term I average two test marks for

2
00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:05.810
forty pupils, on a calculator.

3
00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:09.440
One slip, and a pupil gets the wrong mark.

4
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:11.900
This term my phone does it,

5
00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:13.880
with four lines of Python.

6
00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.670
Today: your first Python programs,

7
00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:20.430
with print, variables and input.

8
00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.830
A program is a list of exact instructions.

9
00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.820
Python runs them one line at a

10
00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:29.580
time, from the top down.

11
00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.940
Here, print shows each message on

12
00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.460
the screen, in this order.

13
00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:37.870
Swap two lines, and the messages swap too.

14
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.320
Now look at the quotes.

15
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.260
With quotes, Python shows the

16
00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.360
text exactly as you typed it.

17
00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:48.070
Without them, it works the sum out first.

18
00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:51.570
So the first line shows seven times six,

19
00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:53.810
and the second shows forty-two.

20
00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:58.060
A variable is a name that holds a value.

21
00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:01.530
The equals sign stores it: the value on

22
00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:03.870
the right goes into the name on the left.

23
00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:06.770
Then you can use the name, and

24
00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:08.250
Python uses its value.

25
00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:10.700
Here the average of fourteen

26
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:12.460
and eighteen is sixteen.

27
00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:15.740
Think of a variable as a

28
00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:17.180
labelled box on a shelf.

29
00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:21.100
The label stays; what’s inside can change.

30
00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:23.550
Put a new value in, and

31
00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:24.790
the old one is gone.

32
00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:28.750
So a variable holds one value at a time.

33
00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:30.530
Quick check!

34
00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:34.260
I print five in quotes,

35
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.960
plus three in quotes.

36
00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:39.630
What appears on the screen?

37
00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:41.770
Fifty-three.

38
00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:45.980
In quotes, five and three are text, and

39
00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:48.760
plus joins text together, like two words.

40
00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:52.540
Without quotes, five plus three is eight.

41
00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:55.690
Text or number: Python always

42
00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:56.730
needs to know which.

43
00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:00.450
To ask a question, use input.

44
00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:04.010
The program waits, and whatever is typed

45
00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:06.550
comes back as text, even a number.

46
00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:09.100
To do sums with it, turn it into

47
00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:10.660
a whole number with int.

48
00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:13.230
Now my program can ask for

49
00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:14.410
any pupil’s marks.

50
00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.360
Small programs like this

51
00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.140
save teachers real time.

52
00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:21.790
Averages for a whole class.

53
00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:25.130
A times table to print for Primary Three.

54
00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:27.260
How many pupils passed.

55
00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:31.580
And it all runs on your phone, offline, in

56
00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:33.740
the same Python that runs on a micro:bit.

57
00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.660
In the app, open Step through a program.

58
00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:41.160
Type your own lines, press Run and

59
00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.080
record, then Next: watch each

60
00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.120
variable appear as its line runs.

61
00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:48.800
Try two marks and their average.

62
00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:50.950
Your turn.

63
00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:54.090
A pupil types twelve when my

64
00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:55.690
program asks for a mark.

65
00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:58.650
What does input give back?

66
00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:03.120
Text, so use int before any sums.

67
00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:06.140
Remember: Python runs top to

68
00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:07.880
bottom, one line at a time.

69
00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:09.740
Quotes make text.

70
00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:12.090
The equals sign stores a

71
00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:13.290
value in a variable.

72
00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:15.980
And input always gives text.

73
00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:18.610
Try it in class tomorrow:

74
00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:20.770
Predict, then run.

75
00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:23.990
Write three short programs on the board.

76
00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:27.400
Pairs write down what each one will show.

77
00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.020
Then run them on your phone and read out

78
00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:32.440
the results: who was right, and why?
